const LESSON = { id:"pharm-antidotes", title:"Antidotes and reversal agents: the must-know pairs", sys:"fund", course:true, topics:["Antidotes and reversal agents"], card:"rc-antidotes", qre:/antidote|reversal|overdose|toxicity/i,
 src:[S(CV,"PDF p. 130 (protamine: 1 mg per about 100 units heparin)"),S(CV,"PDF pp. 142 (vitamin K for warfarin), 148 and 481 (idarucizumab, andexanet alfa), 124 (andexanet), 179 (digoxin immune fab)"),S(PU,"PDF pp. 3 (naloxone), 23 (acetylcysteine), 28 (salicylates: no antidote, sodium bicarbonate)"),S(MAT,"PDF pp. 345 (calcium gluconate at the bedside for magnesium), 348 (vitamin K)"),S(PSY,"Text-blocks 315 (flumazenil and seizure risk), 235 (physostigmine)"),S(MSK,"Text-blocks 409–413 (dantrolene; neostigmine, pyridostigmine, sugammadex for neuromuscular blockers), 459, 464, 513"),S(MS,"PDF pp. 205 (acetylcysteine mixed with soda), 250 (protamine)")],
 hook:"Think of each antidote as a key cut for one lock.\n• Opioids → Naloxone: 'NO more opioids.'\n• Benzodiazepines → Flumazenil: 'FLUMAZEnil wakes the benZOs.'\n• Heparin → Protamine: 'HP' — the Heparin Printer runs on Protamine.\n• Warfarin → Vitamin K: 'W-K' — Warfarin Knocks out clotting, K brings it back.\n• Acetaminophen → Acetylcysteine: A goes with A.\n• Digoxin → Digoxin immune fab: Dig fights Dig.\n• Magnesium → Calcium gluconate: Mag makes you sag; Calcium Comes back.\n• Iron → Deferoxamine: de-FE-roxamine (Fe = iron).\n• Methotrexate → Leucovorin: Leucovorin rescues the leukocytes.\n• Dry drugs (anticholinergics) → Physostigmine; wet poisons (organophosphates) → Atropine + pralidoxime: opposites cancel.\n• Malignant hyperthermia → Dantrolene: Dantrolene douses the muscle fire.\n• Beta blocker → Glucagon. Calcium channel blocker → Calcium.\n• Dabigatran (Pradaxa) → Idarucizumab (Praxbind): Prax for Pradaxa. The '-xabans' (apixaban, rivaroxaban) → Andexanet alfa: An-DEXA-net nets the Xa.",
 simple:"An antidote undoes a specific drug's effect. It buys time, but it never replaces airway, breathing, and circulation. The nurse recognizes the toxicity pattern, stops the drug, supports the ABCs, gets the antidote ordered, and watches for the toxicity to return.",
 patho:"Antidotes work in five ways. 1) They block the receptor (naloxone at opioid receptors, flumazenil at benzodiazepine receptors). 2) They bind the drug so it can't act (protamine binds heparin; digoxin immune fab, idarucizumab, andexanet alfa, and chelators such as deferoxamine bind their targets). 3) They replace what the drug used up or blocked (vitamin K rebuilds clotting factors, leucovorin replaces folate, calcium overrides magnesium). 4) They push the body the opposite way (atropine blocks acetylcholine flooding; glucagon raises heart rate without beta receptors). 5) They rescue a metabolic pathway (acetylcysteine refills glutathione so the liver can detoxify acetaminophen).",
 risk:["Older adults and clients with kidney or liver disease (drugs build up)","Polypharmacy and look-alike drugs","Children: iron tablets, adult medicines left within reach","Intentional overdose","Opioids combined with benzodiazepines or alcohol"],
 findings:["Opioid: pinpoint pupils, RR below 12, sedation, coma","Benzodiazepine: deep sedation, slurred speech; breathing slows mainly when mixed with other depressants","Anticholinergic: hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat (dilated pupils), mad as a hatter, full as a flask (urinary retention), fast heart","Cholinergic or organophosphate: SLUDGE (salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, GI upset, emesis), slow heart, small pupils, wet lungs","Digoxin: nausea, anorexia, yellow-green halos, bradycardia, dysrhythmias; worse with low potassium","Magnesium: loss of deep tendon reflexes, RR below 12, urine below 30 mL/hr, heart block","Heparin or warfarin: bleeding gums, dark stools, hematuria, bruising, high aPTT or INR","Acetaminophen: nausea early, then liver failure at 24–72 hours — the client can look well at first","Iron: vomiting and bloody diarrhea, a deceptive quiet phase, then shock and liver failure","Malignant hyperthermia: rising end-tidal CO2 (often first), jaw or muscle rigidity, tachycardia, then high fever"],
 red:["RR below 10 or unresponsive","Seizure","Wide QRS, heart block, or new dysrhythmia","Bleeding with neuro changes on an anticoagulant","Temperature and end-tidal CO2 rising during or after anesthesia"],
 assess:["ABCs, SpO2, LOC, pupils, DTRs","ECG rhythm and QRS width","What was taken, how much, and when","Labs: aPTT, INR, potassium, glucose, drug levels (digoxin, acetaminophen at 4 hours after ingestion, salicylate, lithium, iron)","Response after the antidote, and again as it wears off"],
 prio:["Airway and breathing first — support ventilation before or while the antidote is drawn up","Stop the offending drug or infusion","Watch for toxicity returning when the antidote is shorter-acting than the drug"],
 interv:{nurse:["Stop the drug or infusion and call for help","Support breathing (bag-mask) for opioid or benzodiazepine overdose while naloxone or flumazenil is given","Naloxone: titrate per order; stay with the client; it lasts 30–90 minutes, often shorter than the opioid — repeat doses may be needed","Protamine: slow IV over about 10 minutes (fast push causes hypotension, bradycardia, anaphylaxis); your CV source: 1 mg neutralizes about 100 units heparin, half the dose if more than 30 minutes have passed","Vitamin K: IV slowly or PO; it takes hours, so for major bleeding expect PCC or FFP too","Magnesium: keep 10% calcium gluconate at the bedside; check reflexes, RR, and urine output before each dose","Acetylcysteine: give within 8–10 hours for the best protection; mask the rotten-egg taste with juice or soda","After digoxin immune fab: monitor potassium (it can drop) — digoxin levels are unreliable afterward","Activated charcoal only if ordered, the client is alert with a protected airway, and the ingestion was recent; it does not bind iron, lithium, alcohols, acids or alkalis, or hydrocarbons","Poison Control: 1-800-222-1222"],orders:["Naloxone, flumazenil, protamine, vitamin K, acetylcysteine, digoxin immune fab, calcium gluconate, glucagon, atropine and pralidoxime, physostigmine, deferoxamine, leucovorin, dantrolene, idarucizumab, andexanet alfa, sodium bicarbonate, phentolamine"],escalate:["No response to the antidote","Seizure, dysrhythmia, or wide QRS","Bleeding that continues after reversal","Re-sedation after naloxone or flumazenil"]},
 meds:[
  ["Opioids → naloxone","IV, IM, intranasal. Short-acting: watch for re-sedation. In opioid-dependent clients, titrate small doses to avoid severe withdrawal and pain."],
  ["Benzodiazepines → flumazenil","IV slowly. Can trigger seizures in long-term benzodiazepine users or mixed overdoses (your psych source). Re-sedation possible."],
  ["Heparin, enoxaparin → protamine sulfate","Slow IV. Fully reverses heparin; only partly reverses enoxaparin. Watch BP and for anaphylaxis (fish allergy, prior NPH insulin use)."],
  ["Warfarin → vitamin K (phytonadione)","Works over hours; PCC or FFP for urgent bleeding. Large doses make the client warfarin-resistant for days."],
  ["Dabigatran → idarucizumab (Praxbind)","For life-threatening bleeding or emergency surgery."],
  ["Apixaban, rivaroxaban → andexanet alfa","Reverses factor Xa inhibitors in life-threatening bleeding."],
  ["Thrombolytics (alteplase) → stop infusion; aminocaproic acid, cryoprecipitate per order","Bleeding or neuro decline during tPA."],
  ["Acetaminophen → acetylcysteine","Best within 8–10 hours; oral or IV."],
  ["Digoxin → digoxin immune fab","For life-threatening toxicity; monitor potassium afterward."],
  ["Magnesium sulfate → calcium gluconate 10%","At the bedside whenever magnesium runs."],
  ["Beta blockers → glucagon (and atropine)","High-dose IV glucagon raises heart rate and contractility without beta receptors."],
  ["Calcium channel blockers → calcium (and glucagon)","Calcium chloride or gluconate per protocol."],
  ["Insulin, sulfonylureas → dextrose or glucagon","15 g fast carbs if awake; IV dextrose or glucagon if not."],
  ["Organophosphates, cholinergic crisis → atropine plus pralidoxime","Atropine until secretions dry; pralidoxime reactivates the enzyme."],
  ["Anticholinergics (atropine, diphenhydramine, benztropine) → physostigmine","Have atropine ready; avoid with tricyclic overdose."],
  ["Acute dystonia from antipsychotics → diphenhydramine or benztropine","IM or IV for fast relief."],
  ["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, malignant hyperthermia → dantrolene","Stop the trigger, cool, dantrolene IV."],
  ["Iron → deferoxamine","Urine turns reddish-orange ('vin rosé'). Charcoal doesn't bind iron."],
  ["Lead → succimer (oral) or calcium disodium EDTA","Chelation; hydrate and monitor kidneys."],
  ["Methotrexate → leucovorin","'Rescue' given on a strict schedule after high-dose methotrexate."],
  ["Tricyclic antidepressants → sodium bicarbonate","Narrows a widening QRS and protects the heart."],
  ["Salicylates (aspirin) → no specific antidote","Sodium bicarbonate alkalinizes the urine; fluids; dialysis if severe (your pain source)."],
  ["Isoniazid → pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Treats INH seizures; also prevents neuropathy."],
  ["Norepinephrine or dopamine extravasation → phentolamine","Stop the infusion; phentolamine infiltrated around the site per order."],
  ["Nitroprusside (cyanide toxicity) → hydroxocobalamin","Turns skin and urine red."],
  ["Serotonin syndrome → cyproheptadine","Stop serotonergic drugs; cool; benzodiazepines for agitation."],
  ["Neuromuscular blockers (rocuronium, vecuronium) → neostigmine with atropine, or sugammadex","Your MSK source: atropine blocks neostigmine's bradycardia and secretions."],
  ["Anaphylaxis → epinephrine IM","First-line; antihistamines and steroids are add-ons."],
  ["Lithium → no antidote","Stop the drug, IV fluids, hemodialysis for severe toxicity."]],
 teach:["Keep naloxone at home whenever opioids are in the house; teach family to use it and call 911","Lock up iron and all medicines; iron overdose is a leading cause of poisoning deaths in young children","Acetaminophen: no more than 4 g/day from all products combined (less with liver disease or alcohol use)","Warfarin: keep vitamin K intake consistent, not zero","Carry an anticoagulant ID card","Poison Control: 1-800-222-1222"],
 eval:{better:["Breathing and LOC return; bleeding stops; rhythm normalizes; labs move toward normal"],worse:["Re-sedation as the antidote wears off; seizure; continued bleeding; new dysrhythmia"]},
 notes:["Course details used here: protamine dosing (CV p. 130); salicylates have no antidote and are treated with sodium bicarbonate (pain file p. 28); calcium gluconate kept at the bedside with magnesium (maternity p. 345); flumazenil seizure risk (psych text-block 315); neostigmine or pyridostigmine with atropine, or sugammadex, for neuromuscular blockers (MSK text-blocks 412–413).","Antidotes not named in your files (deferoxamine, pralidoxime, leucovorin, phentolamine, hydroxocobalamin, aminocaproic acid, succimer) come from standard PN pharmacology references."],
 scenario:"A post-op client on a morphine PCA also received lorazepam for anxiety an hour ago. Now RR is 7, pupils are pinpoint, and he responds only to a sternal rub. SpO2 is 84%.",
 first:{stem:"A client receiving IV morphine is unresponsive with RR 6 and pinpoint pupils. What should the nurse do first?",opts:["Support breathing with a bag-valve mask and call for help","Give flumazenil","Document the findings","Check the client's blood glucose"],key:0,why:"Airway and breathing come first; then give naloxone per order. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids."}};
